WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.380
Kingsman: The Golden Circle didn't run, burst,
or explode into theaters.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.540
It strode in, gentleman-like, to grace our
eyeballs with posh car chases and debonair

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.300
fight scenes.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.860
Director Matthew Vaughn once again went to
outrageous extremes with the action and violence,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.240
pursuing what he called "my Spinal Tap of
trying to find 11 with every scene."

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.640
Polish your Oxfords and drain that martini,
because here are all the Easter eggs you missed

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.240
in Kingsman: The Golden Circle.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.120
Deadly briefcase

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.700
Neither Kingsman movie has been shy about
referencing James Bond, the granddaddy of

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.210
all spy franchises, but The Golden Circle
went a step further and appropriated one of

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.800
007's own weapons for young Eggsy — with
upgrades, of course.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.640
The machine gun briefcase he uses during the
film's climactic raid on Poppy Land was a

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.860
callback to Sean Connery's Bond, who used
a similar weaponized business accessory in

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:49.820
From Russia with Love.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.140
Of course, that one didn't fold out into a
bulletproof shield, but hey, it had a knife.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.040
And speaking of Bond…

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:57.880
Flying colors

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.100
Midway through the film, The Golden Circle
delivers an action-packed sequence in which

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.609
Eggsy and Whiskey find themselves skidding
down a mountain slope in a ski-lift car, barreling

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:09.080
toward a bunch of tourists in the lodge at
the bottom of the mountain.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.560
In a flash of gentlemanly genius, Eggsy pops
Whiskey's parachute to slow them down.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.700
Since Whiskey is a Statesman, the parachute
is the brightest American flag Betsy Ross

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:18.600
ever dreamed of.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.560
Bond lovers probably caught the reference.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.680
In Roger Moore's 1977 The Spy Who Loved Me,
the opening scene has Bond ski off a cliff

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.100
to certain death…only to open his parachute
at the last moment, blazing the colors of

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:30.860
the British flag.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.200
Snowy mountains, a plunge to the death, and
a last-minute parachute emblazoned with stately

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:35.700
pride?

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.400
Definitely a nod to Bond.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.940
Marquee master

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.840
Keeping Elton John as her piano slave is just
one of the villainous Poppy's messed-up moves,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.460
but it gave us one quick Easter egg.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.800
On the marquee outside the theater where she
makes Elton play is a sign advertising Captain

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:54.229
Fantastic, an obscure reference to his album
Captain Fantastic and the Brown Dirt Cowboy.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.280
And if you caught the other side of the marquee,
you probably saw the lettering saying "The

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.100
B---- Is Back," which is an Elton John song.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.300
- Saturday Night's
Alright For Fighting, also makes an appearance

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:06.990
in the film - Elton tweaked it a little in
the film to celebrate Wednesday - the day

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.040
he was getting rescued.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.720
Bennie and Jet

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.220
Poppy's got a real thing going for Elton John
in The Golden Circle.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.680
Not only did she kidnap him — she also named
her dogs for him.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.570
We're talking, of course, about the robo-pooches.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.100
If you watch closely, you can see their names
pop up on screen several times: Bennie and

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:24.640
Jet.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.600
And you might know where this is going — "Bennie
and the Jets" is a song John released in 1973

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.360
on his album Goodbye Yellow Brick Road.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.140
Comic rockets

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.900
The opening chase sequence is a death-defying
spectacle, employing Matthew Vaughn's signature

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.000
frenetic camerawork.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.451
When Eggsy finally gets the go-ahead from
Merlin to return fire on Poppy's goons, he

00:02:43.451 --> 00:02:48.330
does it in the most badass way possible, pushing
a button and sending three rockets flying

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.400
into the air to come back down on the three
vehicles pursuing him.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.260
In the original comic the movie's based on,
The Secret Service, Eggsy uses the same weapon

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.440
to shake murderous pursuers, again in the
opening scene of the story.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.720
Granted, he's on a snowmobile at the time,
and the people chasing him are on skis, but

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.260
the result is still exactly the same: exploded
bad guys.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.640
Deleted scene

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.000
In January 2017, Taron Egerton revealed that
the Kingsman sequel would feature a deleted

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.180
scene from the first movie.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:19.440
He told Den of Geek, it was one he "personally
thought shouldn't have been cut, and that's

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.560
now in the second film.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.600
But what it is…I don't think I can say."

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.760
But now that the movie's out, we can tell
you what scene he was talking about.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.260
Nope, it's not the one with young Michael
Caine.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.060
It's the one when Harry teaches Eggsy how
to eat like a gentleman at breakfast.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.600
In The Golden Circle, the scene shows up when
Eggsy's girlfriend is trying to prepare him

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.320
for dinner with her parents, the king and
queen of Sweden.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.320
So why was it cut?

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.260
"It had a really bad effect because it also
looked like they might have slept with each

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.000
other the night before."

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.660
Heroic reward

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.180
One of the funniest — and most controversial
— scenes in Kingsman: The Secret Service

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.910
came at the end, when the Swedish princess
offered Eggsy a certain sexual favor in return

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:56.740
for saving the world.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.860
Of course, Princess Tilde is back in The Golden
Circle, this time in a much larger role as

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:01.640
Eggsy's girlfriend.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.780
Their relationship has evolved since the 30
seconds of screentime they had together in

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.980
the first film, but Tilde still knows how
to motivate her boyfriend.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.100
Just before he jets off to save the world,
she reminds him what he'll get if he does

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:13.700
a good job.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.540
Considering the backlash against the joke
in the first movie, it took a certain amount

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.400
of moxie for Vaughn to stick another reference
to it in the sequel.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.060
Who says chivalry is dead?

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.200
Thanks for watching!

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.860
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.400
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

